course: dictionary
lesson: 9809
title: Swap and swapping
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To swap is to give one thing and get another back. Zara swapped a gift card for one she could use: «Zara got a gift card for a game she does not even play, felt a little disappointed, thanked her uncle anyway, and swapped it with her mum for one she could use.» Here the card changed hands, and Zara got a different one in return.
explain: Swap can also be a noun: a swap is the act of swapping, or the thing you get. Context tells you which meaning you need. In «In 1817 the economist David Ricardo explained that countries gain by doing what they do best and trading, like swapping spare stickers», the clue is trading: each side gives something and gets something back.
explain: Swap can also mean two people changing places, as in «swap places». To check your meaning, put "give one thing and get another" in place of swap. If it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
fact: To swap means to give one thing and get another in return.
fact: A swap is the act of swapping, or the thing you get.
fact: To swap places means two people change places.
fact: «Swop» is another spelling of swap, used in the UK.
fact: In maths, swapping the order of numbers when you add or multiply does not change the answer: 3 + 5 and 5 + 3 both make 8.
exercise: Zara swapped her gift card with her mum. What does swapped mean here? | Lost it on the way home | Threw it away | Bought a second one for herself | *Gave one thing and got another back
exercise: What might children swap at school? | Sunshine | *Stickers or cards | Teachers | The school building
exercise: In maths, if you swap the order of 3 and 5 when adding, does the answer change? | Yes, it becomes 35 | Yes, it always gets bigger | *No, 3 + 5 and 5 + 3 both make 8 | Only on Fridays
test: «Tom is one of two mates he's swapped, though they still say hi in the corridor.» What does swapped mean here? | *Changed friends, so they are no longer his closest mates | Hid from them in class | Ran away from them | Was given a present by them
test: The economist says countries gain by trading, like swapping spare stickers. What is the main idea? | Countries should never trade | Stickers are worth more than money | *Countries can gain by trading what they do best, as children swap stickers | Swapping means keeping everything for yourself
exam: Which sentence uses swap to mean two people changing places? | The machine swaps force for distance or speed | Zara swapped her gift card for a game | *Please swap places with Sam so you can see the board | Three and five swapped over in the sum
